  "_comment": "SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH for review model pricing, USD per 1M tokens — a DATED table, so it answers both 'what does this cost now?' and 'what did a past run cost?' from one file. Each model maps to a list of windows; a window's rates take effect on `effective` (UTC date, inclusive) and run until the next window. The engine (review.py) bills at the newest window per model; the analysis (tauceti-review-costs) prices each run at the window covering its run date. RULE: a genuine provider PRICE CHANGE adds a new dated window (old runs keep the old rate); a CORRECTION of a wrong past table EDITS the window covering the affected dates (the provider's real rate then wasn't what we'd recorded). input/output = list price (Anthropic: platform.claude.com; OpenRouter-routed incl. gpt-5.5: openrouter.ai). cache_read = prompt-cache input rate (Anthropic ~0.1x input; OpenAI gpt-5.5 = $0.50). Prompt-cache WRITES: Anthropic bills them by TTL, so Claude windows carry cache_write_5m (1.25x input) and cache_write_1h (2x input); OpenAI bills one write rate, so gpt windows carry cache_write (1.25x input). OpenRouter-routed models carry neither: that route reports cache reads only. Optional `long_context` applies the higher tier to the WHOLE request when input_tokens exceed `threshold` (OpenAI's >272K gpt-5.5 tier; its cache_read here is an assumption = 0.1x long-context input, and its cache_write = 1.25x long-context input). Edit here only: TauCetiWorker vendors this file byte-identically as tauceti_worker/prices.json for its own cost model, and its CI fails when the copy drifts from this one.",
